Player: Hey.. uhh...
Player: Are you the Atomon Expert Linos mentioned?
Andrew: May I ask who might you be??
Player: I just got here, and I’m not familiar with this place yet...
Player: Linos said I have to know some stuff, especially about Atomons, like that weird thing I saw...
Player: He called it Hydrogas... what was it again?
Andrew: Ahhh, Hydrubbie. The Hydrogen Atomon that wanders around this place.
Andrew: It’s kind of hard to miss. That little thing is always running around, but never caused trouble wherever it goes.
Player: So... it’s an Atomon?
Andrew: Correct! Atomons are living manifestations of the elements themselves.
Andrew: Each one is associated with an element, and their properties are closely related to it.
Andrew: And by the way, “Atomon” is derived from two words\: “Atomic” and “Monster.” So, Atomon means “Atomic Monster.”
Player: So Hydrubie is... Hydrogen?
Andrew: Precisely! Hydrogen. The lightest element there is.
Andrew: Although don't let its size fool you. Hydrogen may be simple, but it plays a very important role in chemistry.
Player: Chemistry...
Andrew: Ah, yes. You’ll be hearing that word quite often around here.
Andrew: If you're going to travel through Chemistria, you ought to understand the elements and the Atomons that represent them.
Player: I guess I have a lot to learn...
Andrew: Indeed you do!
Andrew: But don't worry. That's what I'm here for.
Player: How did this happen?
Andrew: What happened?
Player: This whole Atomon thing? How did elements become such creatures?
Andrew: You're really clueless, huh? I mean, even the children in this place knows what happened three years ago....
Player: What? What happened back then?
Andrew: It all started on that night....
[signal arg="play_atomon_origin"]
Andrew: ...And that was the beginning of the Atomons.
Player: So that's what happened...
Player: But there's still something I don't understand.
Andrew: What's that?
Player: You said Atomons are connected to elements.
Player: What exactly are elements?
Andrew: Good question.
Andrew: Before you can understand Atomons, you need to understand what they're based on.
Andrew: Chemistry is the study of matter and the changes it undergoes.
Player: Matter?
Andrew: Anything that has mass and takes up space.
Andrew: Everything around you is made of matter.
Andrew: The ground beneath your feet, the water you drink, the air you breathe...
Andrew: Even Atomons are connected to matter and its building blocks.
Player: So Atomons are made of matter?
Andrew: Not exactly.
Andrew: Think of Atomons as living manifestations of the elements.
Andrew: To understand them, you first need to understand atoms.
Player: Atoms?
Andrew: Atoms are the basic building blocks of matter.
Andrew: Different kinds of atoms make up different elements.
Player: So Hydrogen has its own kind of atom?
Andrew: Exactly.
Andrew: Hydrogen is an element because it contains only one type of atom.
Player: And that's why Hydrubbie represents Hydrogen?
Andrew: Precisely.
Andrew: Each element has its own properties.
Player: Properties?
Andrew: Properties are characteristics that help us describe and identify a substance.
Andrew: Some properties can be observed without changing the substance.
Andrew: Those are called physical properties.
Player: Like what?
Andrew: Its appearance, mass, temperature, density, or state of matter.
Player: State of matter?
Andrew: You know them already.
Andrew: Solid. Liquid. Gas.
Player: So Atomons can have different states too?
Andrew: In a way, yes.
Andrew: Look at Hydrubbie
Andrew: Hydrogen normally exists as a gas under ordinary conditions.
Player: So its state is connected to the element it represents?
Andrew: Correct.
Andrew: The properties of an element help determine how its Atomon behaves.
Player: That explains why every Atomon can be different.
Andrew: Exactly.
Andrew: But matter isn't always in the same state.
Andrew: When energy is added or removed, matter can change from one state to another.
Player: Like ice turning into water?
Andrew: That's right.
Andrew: Solid to liquid is melting.
Andrew: Liquid to gas is boiling or vaporization.
Andrew: And gas can become liquid through condensation.
Player: So energy is involved in those changes?
Andrew: Always remember that.
Andrew: Energy is the ability to do work.
Andrew: Matter can gain or lose energy, and that can affect how its particles behave.
Player: Particles?
Andrew: The particles in a solid are packed closely together and mainly vibrate.
Andrew: In a liquid, they can move past one another.
Andrew: In a gas, they move much more freely and are farther apart.
Player: So when matter gains energy, its particles can move more?
Andrew: Exactly.
Andrew: You're starting to understand.
Player: And what about chemical changes?
Andrew: That's another important part of chemistry.
Andrew: In a chemical reaction, atoms are rearranged.
Andrew: The substances you start with are called reactants.
Andrew: The substances formed are called products.
Player: But the atoms don't just disappear?
Andrew: Correct.
Andrew: Matter is conserved.
Andrew: The atoms are rearranged, but they are not simply destroyed or created during an ordinary chemical reaction.
Player: So chemistry is basically about understanding how matter behaves and changes?
Andrew: That's a good way to put it.
Andrew: And that's only the beginning.
Player: There's more?
Andrew: Much more.
Andrew: If you want to travel through Chemistria and understand the Atomons you encounter...
Andrew: You'll need to learn how to identify matter, recognize its properties, and understand how energy affects it.
Player: I think I'm beginning to see why Linos sent me here.
Andrew: Good.
Andrew: Then let's see how much you've actually learned.
[signal arg="quest_chain_001_module_01_complete"]
